83 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cdd9667-323b-5010-a8fc-c007664db1d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055684 #edb/ref 1061 346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c4ae36c-001f-593c-aad4-04fe90c082c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055685 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055685 #edb/ref 1061 348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "feb2aa0a-5b34-5dae-ba30-562950046860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055686 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055686 #edb/ref 1061 350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055686 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c16f856-e95a-5082-937e-48f3494765ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055686 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055954 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055954 #edb/ref 1065 181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4331194-9993-526c-ac4e-85c5017ee722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055954 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055955 #edb/ref 1064 "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055955 #edb/ref 1065 185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055955 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "831603dd-2fca-539d-90bc-81d866ca5d77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055955 #edb/ref 1067 "The Root Rule for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055956 #edb/ref 1064 "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055956 #edb/ref 1065 187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055956 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ec92465-592d-5e08-8d49-a2a3311282c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055956 #edb/ref 1067 "Combining Different Rules" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055982 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following system of equations:\n![](images/01/01eee66dd39797b90503716f097ff604a4f339d8ea0a25ceef9b8ea7a0898ab4.svg)\n\nTo solve systems of equations *graphically*, we first draw each of the equations in the same coordinate system. Then, the solution of the system is given by the common points where the lines intersect.\n\nLet's see how this method works for the system above. To plot the functions given by the equations, we first rewrite them by making ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject in each equation.\n![](images/c7/c7778dd2c82032ef68ad8221a990abe1b2bafb4e36a7a0e2640a43856be58c5a.svg) \n\nThen, we draw the lines in the same coordinate system:\n\n![](images/47/47fb02cf8bb4668b24034302fd4265ec5261f4d244cd9d9d599afeffd91d2424.png)\n\nThe lines intersect at the point ![](images/d8/d87c68d6226f4bb9ecf52ab576a5ac45c83fbb02c83edd1af04ac235dd518467.svg), which is the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/b3/b3b95135ad9a758f891280b15e8bd6f6165bf9f47a76a1c99280af02dad78911.png)\n\nWe can also use the graphical method to solve systems of equations where the corresponding functions are not straight lines. These types of systems are called **nonlinear systems**. Let's consider such an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055982 #edb/ref 1065 3407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055982 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e7d63cd6-92ea-5bb3-adc8-f9d09bf2d24d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055982 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Length of the Hypotenuse of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Legs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059630 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "73ad4cd2-e7f0-5b7d-82fc-1a8e4b0597d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Length of the Legs of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Hypotenuse" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059631 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d5faa905-dcfc-5eed-b4ed-5818ca6ef342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Length of the Diagonal or Side of a Square Given its Perimeter" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059636 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6ae8076e-25bc-5489-ae14-ffa16d17a32c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Length of a Hypotenuse or Leg of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given its Perimeter" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059638 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "16fd63e8-664f-53c2-b623-c696b86b5642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1045 "Isosceles Right Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059938 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3a267dc0-721f-5a1c-ad3a-5fa7fec62f8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060460 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060460 #edb/ref 1061 10416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060460 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b5ee65a-7747-5a0e-9d06-721118b74e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060460 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060461 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060461 #edb/ref 1061 10419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060461 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b3e5ddd-300e-5258-b19c-3499c565bdad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060461 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060462 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060462 #edb/ref 1061 10420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060462 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7049be2a-b0af-5170-a1cb-c440a35c6663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060462 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060468 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060468 #edb/ref 1061 10423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060468 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f44a11e0-a932-51d6-97d6-b216a7d42d5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060468 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060469 #edb/ref 1061 10424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060469 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "247c54c1-1228-55a9-8289-046846fffeeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060469 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060473 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060473 #edb/ref 1061 10430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060473 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "718ecaa7-a103-5a7c-a12a-2815eaf19f82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060473 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060872 #edb/ref 1064 "In isosceles right triangles such as the one shown below, the angles always measure $45^\\circ,$ $45^\\circ,$ and $90^\\circ.$ \n\nConsequently, isosceles right triangles are often called **$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\n![](images/d3/d32159528abc75f3ed8bcccaedb2765b676fd6cc64164e2f8c81f98eb37f6db4.png)\n\nAll $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles share a special property:\n\n*In any ${45^\\circ}$-${45^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.*\n\nTo see why this is, remember that\n\n- in an isosceles triangle, the two legs must have the same length, and\n\n- in a right triangle, the square of the longest side must equal the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides.\n\nSo, if an isosceles right triangle has legs of length $a$ and the hypotenuse has length $h,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =2{a}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060872 #edb/ref 1065 2381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060872 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e5fefd4-4ac4-5999-8c6d-0ad927da9ca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060872 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060873 #edb/ref 1064 "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](images/07/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle). \n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060873 #edb/ref 1065 2382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060873 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e5c49477-841f-56f9-8779-f1a2a1f115cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060873 #edb/ref 1067 "Applications to Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062473 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 1759218606247