ef 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1045 "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cfe67386-85ee-52bc-a19f-9e5f91df386d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1061 178720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f6a809a-5a39-577f-8881-246e8ed35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1061 178721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1825121-20e1-5854-9a35-360f43ffb277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1061 178725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ad80f23-5479-5329-8fd1-39cf7d32c8f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1061 178726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "219a19a2-5ca7-55ec-b664-706e0003f956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1061 178730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54390c19-603c-54bd-bf54-163d31414029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1061 178733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5756f274-95fd-5077-9cb0-2b24aadadb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1064 "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1065 9126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "739ff387-5940-547f-adac-8b2ac8e5a229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1065 9221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "914fb469-510e-5e74-92bc-6174870cbd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1067 "Converting a Line to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105